const chapterData = [
  {
    title: 'What Slope Lens 3D actually does', label: 'PROJECT SCOPE',
    lead: 'A ground-photo decision-support pipeline that links classification, uncertain measurements and a traceable field record.',
    notes: 'Introduce the user and observation, then the actual output. A junior engineer takes a ground-level photo looking up or across a slope. Slope Lens checks the photo, predicts one of six categories, exposes confidence, estimates sizes and adds field guidance. The implementation combines neural features, statistical models and camera geometry. The name includes 3D because the measurement code uses depth and plane geometry; the interactive 3D terrain viewer is still proposed. This website presents the work; it does not load the classifier models or run inference.',
    html: `<div class="reading-columns"><article><h4>The project question</h4><p>Can a ground-level phone photograph support a first-pass assessment of a landslide’s visible type and dimensions? The intended user is a junior engineer observing a slope from a safe position beside a road or in a valley.</p><p>The input is an ordinary photograph with optional distance, camera orientation, location and field observations. The output combines a type distribution, image-quality feedback, estimated measurements, an on-site checklist and a report.</p></article><article><h4>Three different inference tasks</h4><dl class="definition-list"><div><dt>Classification</dt><dd>Which of six visual categories best describes the scene?</dd></div><div><dt>Segmentation</dt><dd>Which pixels belong to the failed material?</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>How do image coordinates translate into physical lengths, areas and volumes?</dd></div></dl></article></div>
      <div class="reading-callout"><b>Implemented versus proposed</b><p>Guided capture, photo checks, classification, size estimation and reporting are implemented in the prototype. Depth estimation and plane geometry are implemented. An interactive 3D terrain viewer is proposed; the presentation’s diagrams are explanatory illustrations.</p></div>
      <h4>Why combine deep learning and geometry?</h4><p>Neural models provide useful image representations, masks and depth estimates. Statistical and geometric stages then combine evidence, choose scale, remove outliers and propagate uncertainty. The technical report describes this as “networks propose, statistics decide.” A visually plausible mask or depth map alone cannot establish accurate metric dimensions.</p>
      <div class="reading-grid three"><article><h5>Phone interface</h5><p>Live framing guide, quality checks, single-photo and site-survey modes, photo sets and selected video frames.</p></article><article><h5>Analysis server</h5><p>Frozen image backbones, linear probability heads, confidence logic, segmentation, depth and robust geometry.</p></article><article><h5>Field record</h5><p>Photo, model confidence, quality, observations, sizes, location context and a saved or shared report.</p></article></div>
      <h4>Scope boundary</h4><p>A photograph cannot reveal an entire failure surface, buried material, groundwater pressures, motion history or hazards beyond the frame. The prototype supports documentation and interpretation. It is not a geotechnical stability analysis, a runout forecast, or a replacement for expert inspection.</p>`,
    refs: [['README.md', 'Application scope and implemented features'], ['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#2-system-architecture', 'Architecture and design principle']]
  },
  {
    title: 'From slope mechanics to a usable field photograph', label: 'FIELD THEORY',
    lead: 'Movement mechanism, visible morphology and photographic quality determine what can be inferred from a single view.',
    notes: 'Distinguish recognising a movement mechanism from predicting stability. The crown, transport zone and toe give a visual narrative, but the app cannot infer pore-water pressure or a factor of safety. The geotechnical stress equation in the study view is background theory, not a model computed by the app. Describe the four survey shots and the two kinds of quality check. The current pipeline can retain a result for an unsuitable photo, marking it not reliable; it does not silently turn a bad photo into a good answer.',
    html: `<div class="reading-columns"><article><h4>Why slopes fail: background theory</h4><p>Failure occurs when the forces driving movement exceed available resistance along a potential surface. A common conceptual relation for shear strength is the effective-stress Mohr–Coulomb expression below.</p><div class="equation">τf = c′ + (σn − u) tan φ′<span>c′: effective cohesion · σn: total normal stress · u: pore-water pressure · φ′: friction angle</span></div><p>Rainfall may increase pore pressure; erosion or excavation can remove support; weathering and discontinuities can weaken a mass. These explain why a slope changes, but the app does not measure those parameters or calculate a factor of safety.</p></article><article><h4>What a photo can show</h4><dl class="definition-list"><div><dt>Crown / head scarp</dt><dd>The upper boundary of visible detachment; curved or planar form can help distinguish slide types.</dd></div><div><dt>Transport zone</dt><dd>The path along which material moved; a channel or planar scar provides process evidence.</dd></div><div><dt>Toe / deposit</dt><dd>Accumulated material, whose texture, sorting, blocks and lobe shape help distinguish falls and flows.</dd></div></dl></article></div>
      <h4>Why a ground-level view?</h4><p>The project is built around an engineer’s phone perspective. Overhead detection benchmarks answer a different visual question: where a scar is visible from above. Ground views expose scarps, faces, deposits and scale objects, while also creating strong perspective and occlusion. Aerial, map and close-up images therefore fall outside the intended input for the type classifier.</p>
      <h4>A four-shot site survey</h4><div class="reading-grid four"><article><h5>Whole slope</h5><p>Preserve crown-to-toe context and the dominant movement.</p></article><article><h5>Scarp</h5><p>Record the detachment shape and visible discontinuities.</p></article><article><h5>Deposit</h5><p>Show material texture and resolvable blocks from a safe position.</p></article><article><h5>Scale object</h5><p>Provide distance or an identifiable reference with known size.</p></article></div>
      <h4>Two independent photo checks</h4><p>The phone evaluates brightness, shadows, highlights, haze, sharpness, resolution and framing-related signals before upload. Pixel thresholds are calibrated from training photos. The server separately averages three learned usable-versus-reject heads. Its usability threshold is 0.5; the report identifies this gate as a weakness, rather than a fully solved safety filter.</p><div class="reading-callout"><b>Current behaviour</b><p>A failed photo check can still produce a visible result, marked “not reliable,” with retake guidance. “No visible landslide” is also different from “unusable photo”: the first is a valid scene category, the second is an input-quality judgement.</p></div>`,
    refs: [['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#6-photo-quality', 'Quality checks and usability gate'], ['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#9-decision-support', 'Four-shot survey and guidance'], ['src/common.py', 'Visible class definitions'], ['https://www.publications.usace.army.mil/Portals/76/Publications/EngineerManuals/EM_1110-2-1902.pdf', 'Background: USACE Slope Stability manual — effective-stress shear strength']]
  },
  {
    title: 'The classification taxonomy and its ambiguities', label: 'MOVEMENT MECHANISMS',
    lead: 'Six practical categories group visual evidence from the Hungr / Varnes tradition; they are not a complete geotechnical diagnosis.',
    notes: 'Show the moving schematics and select a category to discuss its mechanism, field clues and failure modes. Hungr’s full taxonomy includes properties that a photo cannot resolve, so the project groups them into six categories. No visible landslide is a negative class. Reject belongs to the separate usability task. Note the extreme imbalance: 538 no-landslide photos versus 51 earth/mud-flow photos. Per-class recall and precision in the detail panel are test agreement with AI labels, not expert-confirmed truth.',
    html: `<h4>Why six classes instead of the full taxonomy?</h4><p>The source groups related Hungr et al. (2014) types into categories distinguishable from downhill photographs. Properties such as clay sensitivity, saturation and velocity are often invisible. Falls and topples share a category; rock and soil variants of each slide mechanism are grouped; debris flows and earth/mud flows remain separate.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Usable photographs by class and split; test metrics are agreement with AI annotations.</caption><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Train</th><th>Val</th><th>Test</th><th>Test recall</th><th>Test precision</th></tr></thead><tbody>${classData.map(c=>`<tr><th>${c.name}</th><td>${c.train}</td><td>${c.val}</td><td>${c.test}</td><td>${c.recall}%</td><td>${c.precision}%</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
      <div class="reading-columns"><article><h4>Slides versus flows</h4><p>A slide retains a recognisable moving mass along a surface. A flow undergoes more distributed deformation. A rotational slide implies a curved surface and rotation; a translational slide suggests a roughly planar path. A channel, levees and a coarse fan support a debris-flow interpretation, while a fine wet tongue supports earth/mud flow.</p></article><article><h4>Falls versus old talus</h4><p>Angular blocks below a face are evidence of material accumulation, but a single image may not reveal whether movement is fresh. This explains one important confusion: rock fall versus a stable cliff with old talus. Fresh scars and field observations add context unavailable to the classifier.</p></article></div>
      <h4>Labelling rules influence the benchmark</h4><p>Annotators were instructed to label visible evidence and the dominant process, without relying on the caption. If unsure between two landslide types, they assigned reject. This makes the scored type set easier than a collection of all real landslide photographs. A separate hard-case analysis was therefore needed.</p><div class="reading-callout"><b>Hard cases tell a different story</b><p>Among 58 ambiguous landslide photos found in the rejected test set, 29% were rejected by the app, 34% marked uncertain and 36% answered confidently. Only 57% of those confident answers matched the best-guess label. Clear-photo performance should not be generalised to ambiguous scenes.</p></div>
      <h4>Precision and recall answer different questions</h4><p>Recall asks how many examples of a true class are recovered. Precision asks how many predictions of that class are correct. Translational-slide test recall is 53%, compared with 97% for debris flow. Rotational-slide and earth/mud-flow results have only 11 and 8 test examples respectively, so their apparent performance is especially uncertain.</p>`,
    refs: [['labeling_guide.md', 'Taxonomy and labelling rules'], ['report.md#42-untouched-test-split-263-usable-photos-152-sites-resultssummarymd', 'Per-class test metrics'], ['report.md#44-hard-ambiguous-photos-resultshard_casesmd', 'Ambiguous-photo evaluation'], ['https://pubs.usgs.gov/fs/2004/3072/fs-2004-3072.html', 'Background: USGS Landslide Types and Processes']]
  },
  {
    title: 'Dataset construction and leakage control', label: 'EXPERIMENT DESIGN',
    lead: 'An evaluation must test generalisation to new sites, not recognition of another photograph of a familiar event.',
    notes: 'Walk through collect, deduplicate, link sites, fix splits, annotate, and audit. There are 4,954 images, 2,062 sites and 1,325 usable images. Training plus validation contains 1,062 usable images, while the held-out test has 263. Site grouping, not random image splitting, is the key methodological point. All current labels are AI annotations. The technical report explicitly says zero human labels. The poster’s human-labelled wording is not supported by that report. Mention the blind re-look agreement of 63% on 299 flagged train/validation labels as evidence that label noise is material.',
    html: `<h4>A collection designed for the input viewpoint</h4><p>Wikimedia title searches and category walks contributed 3,684 photos; Openverse contributed 1,270, mainly from Flickr. The project reports that available benchmarks it reviewed were overhead imagery and/or detection datasets rather than the required ground-view type task. It therefore assembled a dedicated collection.</p>
      <div class="reading-grid three"><article><h5>Deduplicate</h5><p>A 64-bit difference hash rejects near-duplicates within a Hamming distance of 4.</p></article><article><h5>Link events</h5><p>Original site identifiers, title stems, photo-series dates and DINOv2 cosine similarity ≥ 0.90 join related photos.</p></article><article><h5>Audit separation</h5><p>No site spans splits. A further check searches for usable test photos with near-identical train/validation features.</p></article></div>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Reported frozen dataset. Targets are approximate because entire sites stay together.</caption><thead><tr><th>Split</th><th>All photos</th><th>All sites</th><th>Usable</th><th>Rejected</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Train</th><td>3,314</td><td>1,348</td><td>908</td><td>2,406</td></tr><tr><th>Validation</th><td>632</td><td>304</td><td>154</td><td>478</td></tr><tr><th>Test</th><td>1,008</td><td>410</td><td>263</td><td>745</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h4>Why grouping changes the scientific question</h4><p>A random image split can place two views of the same failure into train and test. The model then benefits from site-specific geology, background and photographic style. Grouping by site asks the harder and more useful question: can a system recognise the movement at a previously unseen place?</p><p>One debris-flow event contributed 132 photos, all in training. Site-balanced evaluation gives each site equal total weight, so that event cannot dominate a shift test merely through its image count.</p>
      <div class="reading-callout"><b>Label provenance is the largest open issue</b><p>All 4,954 labels were assigned by an AI annotator. Two small manual spot checks are described as judgements, not a measured expert-validation score. A blind second look at 299 flagged train/validation labels agreed only 63% of the time. Human relabelling of the test set and inter-annotator agreement remain necessary.</p></div>
      <h4>Training corrections must not leak into test</h4><p>Label-noise correction and model selection are confined to training and validation. The source records an audit in which 9 corrected photos moved into test after site merging; their first-look labels were restored. Models store training IDs and data fingerprints, and evaluation scripts refuse models that have seen a test image.</p>
      <h4>What “phone data” means here</h4><p>The usable set contains 237 EXIF-identified phone photographs, with only 20 in the held-out test. Phone-style augmented copies simulate capture damage, but they cannot replace new field images from actual users and sites.</p>`,
    refs: [['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#3-data', 'Collection, labelling, grouping and splits'], ['report.md#6-audit-2026-10-16-what-a-full-scan-found-and-fixed', 'Leakage audit and corrections'], ['tools/check_integrity.py', 'Integrity checks']]
  },
  {
    title: 'Frozen representations, trained decision boundaries', label: 'MODEL THEORY',
    lead: 'Transfer learning supplies visual representations; class-balanced logistic regression learns a small, regularised decision rule.',
    notes: 'Explain frozen backbones versus trained heads. DINOv2 is self-supervised and represents texture and structure; SigLIP2 is image–text aligned. DINO features concatenate the CLS token and mean patch tokens. Standardisation feeds a multinomial logistic regression with balanced class weights. Each backbone has a usability head and a type head; probabilities are averaged equally. Regularisation C is selected with grouped CV. Explain why this is empirically appropriate for a small dataset: the ensemble beats both full ConvNeXt fine-tuning and the zero-shot Qwen baseline, though the gap to two-model ensembles is small.',
    html: `<div class="reading-columns"><article><h4>What remains frozen?</h4><p>The pretrained image backbone maps a photo x into a feature vector φ(x). Its weights are not updated by the landslide labels. Only lightweight linear heads are fitted. This reduces the amount of task-specific fitting and makes cached embeddings reusable across experiments.</p><p>DINOv2 contributes self-supervised structure and texture features. SigLIP2 contributes an image–text-aligned visual representation. Their complementarity is the motivation for averaging; it is not a guarantee that the ensemble wins on every distribution shift.</p></article><article><h4>Feature dimensions</h4><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>Representation</th><th>Dimensions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>DINOv2-base</th><td>CLS + mean patch</td><td>1,536</td></tr><tr><th>DINOv2-large</th><td>CLS + mean patch</td><td>2,048</td></tr><tr><th>SigLIP2-base</th><td>L2-normalised pooled image</td><td>768</td></tr></tbody></table></div></article></div>
      <h4>The linear head</h4><div class="equation">z = (φ(x) − μ) / σ &nbsp; · &nbsp; p(c | x) = softmax(Wz + b)<span>Standardise each feature, form a linear score for each class, then normalise scores into probabilities.</span></div><div class="equation">min ½‖W‖² + C Σᵢ wᵧᵢ [−log p(yᵢ | xᵢ)] &nbsp; · &nbsp; w<sub>c</sub> = N / (K N<sub>c</sub>)<span>L2 regularisation controls weight size. Balanced class weights prevent frequent categories from dominating the loss.</span></div><p>The implementation uses StandardScaler and multinomial LogisticRegression, with a maximum of 5,000 iterations. C is selected from {0.003, 0.03, 0.3, 1.0} using site-grouped, stratified cross-validation. Smaller C means stronger regularisation in this objective.</p>
      <h4>Two heads per backbone</h4><p>The gate head learns usable versus reject from all 3,946 train/validation photos. The type head learns six classes from the 1,062 usable train/validation photos and their phone-style copies. These answer different questions and use different labels.</p><div class="equation">P(c) = ⅓[Pbase(c) + Plarge(c) + Psiglip(c)]<span>The same equal-weight averaging is applied to the three usability-head probabilities.</span></div>
      <h4>Why not fine-tune the entire network?</h4><p>The fine-tuned ConvNeXt-Tiny baseline has only 908 original training photos. In the reported held-out comparison it reaches 0.665 macro-F1, versus 0.784 for the frozen-feature ensemble. This is evidence for this dataset and protocol, not a universal rule that linear probes outperform fine-tuning.</p>
      <h4>Augmentation without viewpoint destruction</h4><p>Each training photo receives three deterministic phone-style copies: crop, horizontal flip, small tilt, exposure/colour changes, blur, noise, downsampling and JPEG compression. Vertical flips are excluded because uphill/downhill orientation is meaningful. Copies stay in the same CV fold as their source photo.</p>
      <h4>Selection under distribution shift</h4><p>All seven non-empty subsets of the three backbones are compared using the mean site-balanced macro-F1 over grouped CV, Commons→Openverse, non-South-Asia→South-Asia, and camera→phone tests. The three-member ensemble has the highest aggregate score, 0.676, but large+SigLIP performs better on some individual shifts. Selection never uses the held-out test.</p>`,
    refs: [['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#4-classifier', 'Feature extraction, objectives, ensemble and selection'], ['src/probe.py', 'Linear probe implementation'], ['tools/robustness_eval.py', 'Grouped selection and shift evaluation']]
  },
  {
    title: 'Accuracy, selective prediction and the full pipeline', label: 'EVALUATION THEORY',
    lead: 'A confidence threshold changes which questions the system answers. It does not repair a weak input gate or establish expert ground truth.',
    notes: 'Start with the held-out model comparison. Switch to Confidence trade-off and move the slider: the numbers are measured train/validation out-of-fold results, not a simulation or new test. At 0.60, 73% coverage and 88.1% accuracy satisfy the selection rule. At 0.80 accuracy rises to 94.4% but coverage falls below 50%. Return to the denominators: held-out type accuracy 80.6% on all usable images; end-to-end confident usable accuracy 85.7% at 69.2% coverage; all-photo pipeline precision 60.5%. Explain class recall via the third tab. All metrics depend on AI labels and the hard cases were filtered separately.',
    html: `<h4>Accuracy can hide class imbalance</h4><p>Accuracy is the number of correct predictions divided by the number evaluated. Macro-F1 first computes F1 independently for each class, then averages the six values equally. It therefore gives scarce classes a voice alongside the large no-landslide class.</p><div class="equation">Precision<sub>c</sub> = TP<sub>c</sub> / (TP<sub>c</sub> + FP<sub>c</sub>) &nbsp; · &nbsp; Recall<sub>c</sub> = TP<sub>c</sub> / (TP<sub>c</sub> + FN<sub>c</sub>)<br>Macro-F1 = (1/K) Σ<sub>c</sub> 2 Precision<sub>c</sub> Recall<sub>c</sub> / (Precision<sub>c</sub> + Recall<sub>c</sub>)</div>
      <h4>Three denominators that must stay separate</h4><div class="reading-grid three"><article><h5>Type classifier</h5><p><strong>80.6% accuracy</strong> on all 263 usable test photos. Macro-F1 is 0.784, with a site-bootstrap 95% interval of 0.688–0.857.</p></article><article><h5>Confident usable answers</h5><p><strong>85.7% accuracy</strong> when the full pipeline answers confidently on usable test photos; coverage of usable photos is 69.2%.</p></article><article><h5>All-photo system</h5><p><strong>60.5% precision</strong> over confident answers across all 1,008 test photos, including unsuitable uploads.</p></article></div>
      <h4>The selective-prediction rule</h4><div class="equation">u &lt; 0.5: not reliable<br>u ≥ 0.5 and max<sub>c</sub> P(c) &lt; 0.6: uncertain<br>u ≥ 0.5 and max<sub>c</sub> P(c) ≥ 0.6: confident<span>u is mean predicted usability. Uncertain results retain the top two types. These are decision rules, not a certificate of correctness.</span></div><p>The confidence threshold is selected from train/validation cross-validation: choose the smallest candidate reaching at least 85% accuracy among answered photos while covering at least 50% of photos. The selected 0.60 threshold yields 88.1% accuracy at 73% coverage before the photo gate.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Reported train/validation out-of-fold risk–coverage results, before the photo gate.</caption><thead><tr><th>Threshold</th><th>Coverage</th><th>Answered accuracy</th><th>Macro-F1</th></tr></thead><tbody>${riskData.map(r=>`<tr${r.threshold===0.6?' class="highlight-row"':''}><th>${r.threshold.toFixed(2)}</th><td>${r.coverage}%</td><td>${r.accuracy}%</td><td>${r.f1.toFixed(3)}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
      <h4>Why the all-photo result is lower</h4><p>The test contains 745 rejected photos and 263 usable ones. The gate accepts 83.3% of usable photos and rejects 83.8% of unsuitable ones. Nevertheless, 29.5% of all confident answers are on photos that should have been rejected. A good six-way classifier cannot compensate for every bad input accepted upstream.</p>
      <h4>Uncertainty in the evidence</h4><p>Bootstrap intervals resample sites so correlated photographs are not treated as independent evidence. Real phone test data contain only 20 photos (60% accuracy; 0.526 macro-F1). Artificial degradation is informative but uses the same recipe as training, making it a favourable stress test. All reported class scores measure agreement with AI annotations.</p>
      <div class="reading-callout"><b>Model confidence is not slope safety</b><p>A high maximum class probability is a statement about the model’s category distribution. It is not the probability that a slope is safe, nor proof that the predicted category is correct under a new region, camera or capture condition.</p></div>`,
    refs: [['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#45-decision-rule-predictordecide', 'Decision rules and threshold table'], ['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#5-evaluation', 'Metrics, held-out and system evaluation'], ['report.md#44-hard-ambiguous-photos-resultshard_casesmd', 'Hard-case limitations']]
  },
  {
    title: 'From image pixels to uncertain physical dimensions', label: 'CAMERA GEOMETRY',
    lead: 'Segmentation identifies the material, depth provides shape, scale supplies metres, and geometry defines what is measured.',
    notes: 'Use the stage buttons to explain the measurement pipeline, then switch to Actual output to anchor the theory in the poster. The camera equation back-projects a pixel using focal length and depth. A typed distance or known object helps resolve scale. RANSAC fits a slope plane despite outliers. Area requires a viewing-angle correction; volume additionally depends on a pre-failure surface or empirical law. Open the scale illustration in the theory panel: doubling geometric scale doubles length, quadruples area and multiplies geometric volume by eight. It is a teaching example, not recomputed project output. Reported 90% ranges are model uncertainty ranges, with field coverage still unverified.',
    html: `<h4>The pinhole camera model</h4><p>A pixel gives a ray, not a physical distance. With focal length f in pixels, principal point (cx, cy), and depth z, the pixel is back-projected into a 3D point:</p><div class="equation">P(u,v,z) = ((u − cx)z/f, (v − cy)z/f, z)<br>ℓ = z × ℓpx / f<span>At a fixed focal length, the same pixel extent corresponds to a larger object when the depth is larger.</span></div>
      <div class="reading-columns"><article><h4>Depth and outline</h4><p>Depth Anything V2 and Depth Pro produce metric-depth estimates with augmentation. Log-space inverse-variance fusion reduces the influence of unstable outputs and adds model disagreement to the variance. CLIPSeg, a self-trained patch head and SAM propose the outline; graph cut favours image/depth boundaries while a vegetation/sky prior suppresses irrelevant regions.</p></article><article><h4>Scale evidence, in priority order</h4><ol><li>Typed distance to the slope.</li><li>Walk-along triangulated points from a known baseline.</li><li>Detected objects with approximate known heights.</li><li>Field calibration, when measured data become available.</li><li>A corrected depth-ensemble prior.</li></ol><p>Every source has uncertainty. Person and vehicle sizes are priors, not exact rulers.</p></article></div>
      <h4>Why scale uncertainty dominates</h4><div class="scale-lab" data-scale-lab><div><label>Illustrative scale multiplier <output data-scale-value>1.00×</output><input type="range" min="50" max="200" step="5" value="100" aria-label="Illustrative geometric scale multiplier" data-scale-input></label><p>Normalised geometry only. This does not recompute the poster’s estimates or the project’s class-dependent volume laws.</p></div><div class="scale-values"><div><span>Length</span><strong data-scale-length>1.00×</strong></div><div><span>Area</span><strong data-scale-area>1.00×</strong></div><div><span>Geometric volume</span><strong data-scale-volume>1.00×</strong></div></div></div><p>The shared scale error makes physical dimensions correlated. Under a uniform 3D scale change s, length grows as s, area as s² and geometric volume as s³. The project also uses empirical volume laws, so its final volume uncertainty includes more than pure geometric scaling.</p>
      <h4>Robust plane geometry</h4><p>RANSAC fits a plane to the back-projected mask points, then refits its inliers with SVD. An across-slope axis and a gravity-oriented down-slope axis define measurements. Lengths use the 2nd–98th percentile extent to reduce sensitivity to stray points. Area sums pixel footprints corrected for the angle between the viewing ray and plane normal.</p>
      <h4>How volume is estimated</h4><p>For slides, a published area–volume law V = 0.074 A^1.45 is one source. Flows and falls use area × a class-dependent thickness prior. A thin-plate spline fitted to surrounding intact ground proposes a pre-failure surface. The surface and law estimates are combined in log space with uncertainty-dependent weights.</p>
      <h4>Monte Carlo and honest ranges</h4><p>1,000 draws vary outline variants and a shared scale factor, alongside volume assumptions. The app reports P50 and P5–P95, plus size-class probabilities. Several photos of one site are fused by medians and between-photo spread; correlated errors prevent an automatic 1/√n reduction.</p><div class="reading-callout"><b>Validation still missing</b><p>No surveyed field sizes exist. The current object-prior refit has a predictive log standard deviation of 0.536; older v4 interval-width tables used 0.345 and need a new evaluation. Outline validation uses teacher pseudo-labels. Grain D50 ranges omit the full scale error, and the report lists inactive field-calibration bugs that must be fixed before collecting calibration data.</p></div>`,
    refs: [['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#7-size-estimation', 'Camera, depth, scale, geometry, volume and uncertainty'], ['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#15-known-weaknesses', 'Outstanding measurement limitations'], ['src/dim_stats.py', 'Statistical and geometry implementation']]
  },
  {
    title: 'Field integration, software boundaries and the next experiment', label: 'ENGINEERING & VALIDATION',
    lead: 'A practical field tool must guide capture, preserve uncertainty and make the next validation step concrete.',
    notes: 'Close by connecting the model to the actual user workflow. The app returns type first, estimates sizes afterward and allows field questions, annotations and reports. Bayesian questions use expert likelihoods, not a new learned ground-truth source. Photo sets handle turning and walking differently, with evidence currently based on simulation. Prioritise blind human labels, 5–10 measured sites and 30–50 phone photos, gate calibration, then measurement correctness and 3D viewer work. A public presentation site is much lighter than the actual Python inference server; this website is not a deployed classifier.',
    html: `<h4>Guidance is part of the pipeline</h4><p>The app moves through safe-position reminders, framing, immediate quality feedback, type prediction, field checks and reporting. It supports a four-shot site survey, locally queued uploads when connectivity fails, and saved records. Type can return before the slower measurement stage; the user can review signs while sizes are computed.</p>
      <h4>Bayesian field questions</h4><p>When the model is uncertain or its top two classes are close, yes/no questions ask about features such as a curved scarp, planar surface, channel, levees or fine material. An answer updates the model distribution using expert-rule likelihoods:</p><div class="equation">P(c | yes) ∝ P(c) P(yes | c)<br>q* = argmaxq [H(P) − Eanswer H(P | answer)]<span>Choose the question with greatest expected information gain. Likelihoods are bounded heuristics, not learned field evidence.</span></div><p>The updated field probabilities appear alongside the model probabilities. Answers can be undone. Questions stop after five answers, negligible expected information gain, or a sufficiently dominant top class.</p>
      <h4>Photo sets: turning and walking are different</h4><div class="reading-columns"><article><h5>Turn on the spot</h5><p>RootSIFT correspondences and robust homographies join overlapping views. For a pure rotation, H = KRK⁻¹ links the camera model to the mosaic. The set classifier assigns weight 0.25 to the mosaic and 0.75 to the mean of individual-photo probabilities.</p></article><article><h5>Walk along</h5><p>A fundamental/essential-matrix route uses parallax, camera pose and triangulation. A baseline estimated as steps × 0.75 m provides scale. Reported stereo success is 71% on simulated pairs; field validation and proper stride forwarding remain open.</p></article></div><p>Video selection keeps sharp, overlapping frames on the phone, then uploads those frames as an ordinary photo set. Full videos are not uploaded. Auto-sweep and keyframe evidence in the report is synthetic, not a completed real-phone field trial.</p>
      <h4>A deployment distinction</h4><p>This presentation is a static website with local assets and no inference dependency. The classifier is a separate Python service with pretrained backbones, learned heads, model downloads and substantially higher memory and compute requirements. Publishing the deck does not publish the analysis server.</p>
      <h4>Prioritised next experiment</h4><ol class="roadmap"><li><b>Establish trusted labels.</b> Blind human re-labelling of the 263 usable test photos, with independent reviewers and Cohen’s κ where available.</li><li><b>Measure in the field.</b> Collect 30–50 local phone photos at 5–10 sites with tape or rangefinder reference measurements.</li><li><b>Repair the weak links.</b> Calibrate the photo gate using out-of-fold predictions; fix field-calibration and stride handling; include scale in grain-size ranges.</li><li><b>Re-run uncertainty evaluation.</b> Use the current scale prior, evaluate interval coverage and document failures by class and capture condition.</li><li><b>Extend after validation.</b> Better multiview geometry and the proposed interactive 3D terrain viewer.</li></ol>
      <div class="reading-callout"><b>What a successful next version would demonstrate</b><p>Agreement with independent human labels, reliable handling of unsuitable photos, useful coverage on real local phone images, and measured physical quantities falling inside stated ranges. More polished output alone cannot demonstrate those outcomes.</p></div>`,
    refs: [['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#8-photo-sets-and-video', 'Multiview and keyframe pipeline'], ['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#9-decision-support', 'Bayesian field questions'], ['TECHNICAL_REPORT.md#16-improvement-roadmap', 'Prioritised validation roadmap']]
  }
];
